d thing is log everything  print out metrics  uh um so track the training lots and the  validation lots over time make sure that  it's going down as intended record the  hyper parameters  that you're using to train so you can  keep track of what you actually did to  get through your result for now  statistics of the data set how many  features how many examples um the model  uh how many weights are there the norm  of the weights predictions as you saw  and it was really useful to have this  file that showed you  exactly how the model makes each  prediction it just gives you a lot more  insight  finally  uh spend some time figuring how to  organize your experiments i like to have  each run i make go into a separate  folder which you can save  um  and so then later back later you can go  back and check  all the models the predictions and  a record of all the hyper parameters  that you use so that you just have an  idea of what you did  and then a note about reporting your  results  it's important to run your experiments  multiple times  particularly with different random seeds  so you make sure that your  results are stable and reliable and then  you can report the mean and the standard  deviation over  these random seeds  and finally um  we often in machine learning tend to be  guilty of distilling everything down  into one number as a test error  um but in in practice we might be  interested in multiple metrics in  particular it's important to make sure  that  if you get five percent error understand  what those errors are sometimes it's  useful to report the error rates on  different  minority groups or subpopulations if you  have access to that information  and generally be cognizant of the biases  in your model  okay to summary summarize we've talked  about the practice of machine learning  first make sure you have good data  hygiene  separate your test set leave it alone  and divide your training set into a  validation and the rest don't look at  the touch set  